4 skills discovered
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: green
REAL_USE_CASE: LLM serving operators (e.g., Anthropic Claude Code, OpenAI Codex teams) allocating prefill vs decode resources for agent workloads; system builders (vLLM, SGLang, TensorRT-LLM, NVIDIA Dynamo) prioritizing optimization targets for agentic inference
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Real win, not microbench: 63.3% CC / 86.1% Codex tool-result token mass (session-clustered LB95 0.502 / 0.845) establishes prefill (not decode) as the dominant cost center for agents. Heavy-tail (CC top-10% results carry 78.8% mass, Gini 0.864) identifies cacheable hot spots. Survives production because it's token-volume accounting independent of kernel speed; reframes DistServe 2401.09670/Sarathi-Serve 2403.02310/vLLM-APC 2309.06180 resource allocation.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [Low: measurement guides existing prefill-optimization investments rather than requiring new mechanisms; validates re-balancing from decode to prefill. Risk: CC LB95 0.502 is near 0.50 threshold (Codex 0.845 solid). Cross-instrument replication (CC+Codex) required by RE-A3.]
RATIONALE: PICK A. Charter A measures 63.3% (CC) and 86.1% (Codex) tool-result token dominance with LB95 0.502/0.845, establishing prefill as the agent serving cost center versus chat's decode focus. This directly impacts capacity planning for prefill/decode disaggregation (DistServe 2401.09670) and prefix caching (vLLM-APC 2309.06180, RadixAttention). Charter C's 89.3-100% file staleness (LB95 0.893/1.000) is decisive but narrower scope (file-read subset); the prefill-mass finding reframes entire serving stack economics with actionable negative ("decode-balanced like chat" if LB95 crosses 0.50).
